A foolproof Dutch oven method for tender, juicy pulled pork. The spiced brown sugar dry rub permeates the meat overnight, followed by a hard stovetop sear and a gentle 5- to 6-hour oven braise.

*Adapted from [Martha Stewart](https://www.marthastewart.com/956163/braised-pulled-pork-shoulder).*

## Ingredients

- 8 to 10 pound bone-in Boston butt pork shoulder
- 3 tablespoons packed light-brown sugar
- 2 tablespoons coarse kosher salt
- 1 teaspoon freshly ground black pepper
- 2 teaspoons paprika
- ½ teaspoon dry mustard
- ½ teaspoon ground cumin
- ½ teaspoon cayenne pepper
- 2 tablespoons vegetable oil
- 2 cups water (or chicken broth)
- Barbecue sauce, for serving
- Soft potato buns or brioche rolls, for serving

## Instructions

1. **Spice Rub & Cure:** Set the pork shoulder on a rimmed baking sheet. In a small mixing bowl, stir together the brown sugar, coarse salt, black pepper, paprika, dry mustard, ground cumin, and cayenne. Rub this aromatic seasoning mix firmly into every crevice of the pork. Wrap the roast tightly in plastic wrap and allow it to marinate in the refrigerator for a minimum of 2 hours, or preferably overnight to let the cure penetrate.
2. **Bring to Room Temp & Sear:** Preheat your oven to 325°F (165°C). Remove the pork from the fridge 30 minutes before cooking to take the chill off, then dab away exterior moisture with paper towels so it browns properly. Heat the oil in a large Dutch oven over medium-high. Carefully sear the roast on all sides until a deep, flavorful mahogany crust forms (around 4 minutes per side).
3. **Braise Low and Slow:** Pour 2 cups of water around the pork into the Dutch oven. Position the shoulder so the fat cap faces up. Seal tightly with the lid and slide into the oven. Braise for 5 to 6 hours, uncovering briefly once an hour to ladle braising liquid over the top. The roast is ready when a carving fork twists effortlessly through the meat.
4. **Shred and Dress:** Move the roast onto a carving board. Slip out the blade bone (it should pull away cleanly) and trim any unrendered surface fat. Using two forks or meat claws, shred the pork into juicy shreds. Drizzle with a few ladles of the defatted pan broth to keep it succulent, or fold in warm barbecue sauce and ladle onto soft potato rolls.

## Notes

- **Make-Ahead:** Pulled pork reheats wonderfully. Store shredded meat in the refrigerator submerged in its strained braising juices; reheat gently on the stovetop or in a slow cooker.
